  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: High pressure remains anchored across the\nDesert Southwest which will keep temperatures above normal this\nweek. There is however shortwave energy passing eastward through \nthe Pacific Northwest and Intermountain West at this time. Impacts\nfrom this passing system will be 3 to 5 degrees of cooling across\nnorthern Nevada compared to yesterday, with high temperatures in \nlocations such as Winnemucca and Elko in the low to mid 90s this \nafternoon. Central Nevada will see a more subtle response given \ncloser proximity to the Southwest ridge; locations such as Ely and\nTonopah can expect around 1 to 3 degrees of cooling with afternoon \nhighs today in the mid 90s. The passing trough will also produce \nbreezy northerly winds of 5 to 15 mph gusting 15 to 25 mph in \nvalleys of Humboldt and Elko Counties early this morning, with \ngusts to 30 mph over the higher terrain. Winds across central \nNevada early this morning will be lighter, generally in the 5 to \n10 mph range. Late this morning and afternoon with the trough to \nour north exiting into Wyoming winds will weaken across northern \nNevada. Most valley locations across both northern and central \nNevada can expect winds this afternoon generally in the 7 to 12 \nmph range with gusts of 15 to 20 mph. \n\nHigh pressure strengthens Tuesday across the Desert Southwest and\nalong the west coast, kicking off a warming trend across northern\nand central Nevada. High temperatures Tuesday will generally be in\nthe mid 90s across the region, with upper 90s to low 100s\nWednesday. Temperatures are expected to gradually trend warmer\ninto Thursday and Friday with most valley locations at or below\n5500 feet in elevation reaching 100 degrees each afternoon. \n\nWednesday afternoon under the building high pressure mid-level \nmoisture will being to push up into east-central NV (Northeastern\nNye and Southern White Pine Counties). At this time moisture and \ninstability look to be too limited for thunderstorm development, \nhowever afternoon and early evening cumulus cloud build-ups at \naround 15k feet are becoming more likely. Cumulus build-ups will \nlikely trend farther north each afternoon, extending into Elko \nCounty by the weekend. Any storm activity that may develop later \nthis week should develop across east-central NV first, and expand\nnorthward as mid-level moisture and instability gradually \nincrease from the south. Current thunderstorm chances for east- \ncentral NV are around 5% both Wednesday and Thursday afternoons, \nincreasing to around 10% Friday and up to 15% on Saturday. The \nprobability of thunderstorms is lower the farther west and north \nof east-central NV that we look each afternoon. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in cooler\nconditions today, with a warming trend Tuesday into the latter\npart of the work week; with highs reaching upper 90s and low 100s\nThursday through at least Saturday. High confidence in dry\nconditions through mid-week. Low confidence in thunderstorm \ndevelopment Wednesday into the weekend. No chance to the NBM\nforecast.",
